From: Bastiaan van der Plaat <bastiaan.v.d.plaat@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:09:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Keep brush models and skins out of RAM

Mod_LoadModel read the whole .bsp into temporary hunk memory before
parsing it, which is more than 1 MiB for most maps and most of what the
DS has. Brush models are now read from the file a lump at a time, only
one lump in temporary memory. Alias and sprite models are small and
still load in one piece.

The 3D hardware keeps its own copies, so the rest doesn't stay in RAM:
textures are only read up to their headers, the renderer reads their
pixels into VRAM (R_LoadTextures), alias skins stay in the model file,
which the renderer reads again when a map starts, and the vertexes,
edges, surfedges and light maps go to the high hunk and are freed once
R_BuildSurfaces made the hardware vertices and vertex light out of them.
The planes aren't allocated twice. The temporary memory a file was read
into goes back to the cache once it's used (Hunk_FreeTemp).
